# Python Coding Best Practices for Cursor IDE

## General Python Guidelines
- Follow PEP 8 style guide strictly
- Use type hints for all function parameters and return values
- Prefer explicit over implicit (Zen of Python)
- Keep functions small and focused (single responsibility principle)
- Use meaningful variable and function names following snake_case convention
- Classes should use PascalCase

## Code Style
- Use 4 spaces for indentation (no tabs)
- Maximum line length: 88 characters (Black formatter default)
- Use f-strings for string formatting (Python 3.6+)
- Prefer list comprehensions and generator expressions when appropriate
- Use context managers (`with` statements) for resource management

## Documentation
- Write docstrings for all classes, functions, and modules
- Use Google-style docstrings with Args, Returns, Raises sections
- Include docstrings even for private methods (prefixed with single underscore)
- Add type information in docstrings for complex return types

## Error Handling
- Use specific exception types instead of bare `except:` clauses
- Always handle exceptions gracefully with proper error messages
- Use `sys.exit()` with appropriate exit codes in CLI scripts
- Log errors appropriately (consider using logging module for production code)
- Provide meaningful error messages to users

## Imports
- Sort imports alphabetically within each group
- Avoid wildcard imports (`from module import *`)
- Place imports at the top of the file (after module docstring)

## Classes and Functions
- Keep classes focused on a single responsibility
- Use `__init__` for initialization, keep it simple
- Prefer composition over inheritance when appropriate

## CLI Scripts
- Use argparse for command-line argument parsing in complex scripts
- Provide helpful usage messages
- Validate input parameters early
- Use `if __name__ == "__main__":` guard
- Exit with appropriate exit codes (0 for success, non-zero for errors)

## Security
- Never hardcode secrets or API keys
- Use environment variables or secure configuration management
- Validate and sanitize user inputs
- Use parameterized queries when interacting with databases

## Code Organization
- Group related functionality into modules
- Keep main execution logic in `if __name__ == "__main__":` blocks
- Separate business logic from I/O operations
- Use helper functions to break down complex operations

## Comments and Documentation
- Write self-documenting code (clear names > comments)
- Use comments to explain "why" not "what"
- Keep comments up-to-date with code changes
- Remove commented-out code (use git history instead)

## Specific Patterns for This Project
- For AWS Lambda metrics: use paginators, handle missing data gracefully
- For GitHub API calls: handle rate limiting, validate responses
- For markdown generation: use consistent formatting
- For date handling: use datetime objects, format consistently (DD-Mmm-YY or similar)
- Use meaningful default values for optional parameters

## Code Quality
- Keep functions under 50 lines when possible
- Keep classes under 300 lines when possible
- Avoid deep nesting (max 4 levels)
- Refactor duplicated code into reusable functions
- Use meaningful variable names that indicate purpose

